                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial^h \circ \partial^h = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commute
          %p
            This means that a
            %strong double complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex"} complex
            in a category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexes"} complexes
          %p
            Accordingly, a
            %strong double chain complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ,
                      %mo •
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi h
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X_{\bullet, \bullet} =
                  \\left[
                  \\cdots \to X_{n,\bullet} \stackrel{\partial^h}{\to} X_{n-1,\bullet} \to \cdots
                  \\right]
                  \\,.
          %p
            In the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"} direct sums
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+complex"} total complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Tot
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Tot X)_\bullet
            associated to a double complex, which in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is the direct sum of all terms of
            %em total degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Tot
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mi l
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (Tot X)_n \coloneqq \oplus_{k+l = n} X_{k,l}.
          %p Often it is such total complexes that are of interest.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            of the total complex is the sum of the horizontal and the vertical differential
            = succeed "." do
              %em made anti-commutative by adjusting signs
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
          %p
            Double chain complexes often arise from the application of bifunctors –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functors"} additive functors
            of two variables – of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1, C_2, C_3
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F :  C_1 \times C_2 \to C_3
          %p
            to complexes in their two arguments. Combining this with the formation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+complexes"} total complexes
            then yields bifunctors from categories of complexes to categories of complexes.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde F : Ch(C_1) \times Ch(C_2) \to Ch(C_3)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The most important examples of this are induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            functor together with their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext"} Ext
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tor"} Tor
          %p
            Notice that under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            and with sufficient
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolutions"} resolutions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde F
            can be understood as the internal hom or tensor products, etc., between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} higher groupoids
          %p
            Although we suggest (and prefer) the ‘complex of complexes’ definition, as above, rather than the equivalent
            %em anticommutiing diagram
            one, we give both and will discuss how to go between them in some detail.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#with_commuting_differentials With commuting differentials
          %p
            A double complex,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ X_{p,q} \mid p,q\in \mathbb{Z} \}
          %p
            and with two classes of ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentials"}> differentials
            ’ or ‘boundary morphisms’:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                      %mi v
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X^v: X_{p,q}\to X_{p,q-1}
              called the ‘vertical boundary morphisms’ or ‘vertical differentials’, with
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi d
                        %mi X
                        %mi v
                      %msubsup
                        %mi d
                        %mi X
                        %mi v
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X^v d_X^v = 0
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                      %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X^h: X_{p,q}\to X_{p-1,q}
              called the ‘horizontal boundary morphisms’ or ‘horizontal differentials’, with
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
