                                %mi X
                                %mi v
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \vdots &amp;&amp; \vdots
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v}
                  \\\
                  \\cdots &amp;\to &amp;
                  X_{n,m} &amp;\stackrel{\bar{d}_X^h}{\to}&amp; X_{n,m-1}
                  \&amp; \to &amp; \cdots
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v} &amp; \swArr_{-1} &amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v}
                  \\\
                  \\cdots &amp;\to &amp;
                  X_{n-1,m} &amp;\stackrel{\bar{d}_X^h}{\to}&amp; X_{n-1,m-1}
                  \&amp; \to &amp; \cdots
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{d_X^v}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \vdots &amp;&amp; \vdots
                  }
          %h3#EquivalenceOfTheTwoDefinitions Equivalence of the two definitions
          %p Which definition is ‘better’? ‘Commuting squares’, i.e., the first version, is convenient if you want to define a double complex as a chain complex in the category of chain complexes. On the other hand, ‘anticommuting squares’ and version 2 is sometimes convenient for defining the total complex (for computing total homology). Does it matter which you use? The following says they are just two views of the same situation.
          %p
            One makes a double complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with commutative squares into a double complex with anticommutative squares by using the same vertical differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^v
            but taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{d}^h : X_{p,q} \to X_{p,q-1}
            to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi p
                    %msup
                      %mi d
                      %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)^p d^h
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#total_complex_of_a_double_complex Total complex of a double complex
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+complex"} total complex
            of a double complex (under the convention that squares commute) is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi tot
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tot_{\oplus}^k = \bigoplus_{m+n=k} X_{n,m}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi tot
                        %mo ⊕
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                    %mi v
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d^k_{tot_\oplus}|_{X_{n,m}} =
                  d^v_X + (-1)^\bullet d_X^h
          %p Similarly, one can define the product total complex as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi tot
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  tot_{\prod}^k = \prod_{m+n=k} X_{n,m}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi tot
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ,
                          %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                    %mi v
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d^k_{tot_\prod}|_{X_{n,m}} =
                  d^v_X + (-1)^\bullet d_X^h
          %p
            Note that these two coincide when the set of non-zero objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{n,m}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n + m = k
            is finite, for example, when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a first quadrant double complex.
          %h3#fundamental_lemmas Fundamental lemmas
          %p
            There is series of basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lemmas"} lemmas
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            which determine the horizontal/vertical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            of a double complex in some row or column from exactness information in other columns. The most fundamental of these is maybe the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/salamander+lemma"} salamander lemma
          %p from which a series of others follow:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/snake+lemma"} snake lemma
                (see also
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connecting+homomorphism"} connecting homomorphism
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequence+in+homology"} long exact sequence in homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3x3+lemma"} 3x3 lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/5-lemma"} 5-lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horseshoe+lemma"} horseshoe lemma
                \.
          %h2#relation_to_homotopy_colimits Relation to homotopy colimits
          %p
            The total complex of the double complex induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
            is a model for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
            of that map, see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "mapping+cone#ConeViaDoubleComplex"} mapping cone – via double complexes
              for more.
